#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082985 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dccdc3ad-6acb-57ae-83ef-95ffedfbff2f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Velocity Vector of a Particle Moving in Two Dimensions"}] :question/id #uuid "9b28994b-b161-504b-8993-d300f464a2cc" :question/math-academy-id "e-616" :question/problem "The position vector $\\mathbf{r}$ of a particle $P$ relative to a fixed origin $O$ at time $t > 0$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\langle 3t^3, 2t^2\\rangle.\n$$\n\nCalculate the velocity vector $\\mathbf{v}.$" :question/worked-solution "The particle's velocity vector is given by the derivative of its position vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{3}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨9{t}^{2},\\,4t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082986 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ca363e2-c7b7-5196-bd38-10bcc30123ce" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Velocity Vector of a Particle Moving in Two Dimensions at a Given Time"}] :question/id #uuid "768aa1ca-15ff-5307-8184-6dd207f7e061" :question/math-academy-id "e-617" :question/problem "The position vector $\\mathbf{r}$ of a particle $P$ (relative to a fixed origin $O$) at time $t > 0$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\langle 12t, 2t^3\\rangle.\n$$\n\nCalculate the velocity vector $\\mathbf{v}$ at time $t=1.$" :question/worked-solution "The particle's velocity vector is given by the derivative of its position vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(12t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{3})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨12,6{t}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the velocity when $t=1$, we substitute into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(1) & =⟨12,6(1)^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨12,6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082987 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "646467d1-f764-5a3c-b03b-7d74e588611d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Speed of a Particle at a Given Time"}] :question/id #uuid "b9a41b06-e359-5e55-bad3-37922a87db14" :question/math-academy-id "e-618" :question/problem "The position vector $\\mathbf{r}$ of a particle $P$ relative to a fixed origin $O$ at time $t > 0$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\langle 6t^2+2, 2t^3\\rangle.\n$$\n\nCalculate the speed of the particle at time $t=2.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the velocity vector $\\mathbf{v}.$ Differentiating the position vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6{t}^{2}+2),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{3})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨12t,6{t}^{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the velocity when $t=2$, we substitute into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(2) & =⟨12(2),6(2)^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨24,24⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the speed at $t=2$ we calculate the magnitude of $\\mathbf{v}(2),$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|v(2)| & =\\sqrt{{24}^{2}+{24}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2({24}^{2})} \\\\  & =24\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the speed when $t=2$ is $24\\sqrt{2}\\approx 33.94$ to two decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082988 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "11a24997-1f1e-5968-a331-980ca14be2f6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Times at Which a Particle Is Stationary"}] :question/id #uuid "c90c307e-39b7-5159-95bc-9d2961369396" :question/math-academy-id "e-619" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves in the $x$-$y$ plane (relative to a fixed origin $O$) with position vector\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\langle t^2-2t, t^3-3t \\rangle, \\quad t\\in[0,\\infty).\n$$\n\nCalculate the times at which the particle is stationary." :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the velocity vector $\\mathbf{v}.$ Differentiating the position vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}-3t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t-2,\\,3{t}^{2}-3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle is stationary when its velocity is zero. This gives,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =0 \\\\ ⟨2t-2,3{t}^{2}-3⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we need\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2t-2 & =0\\,⇒\\,t=1, \\\\ 3{t}^{2}-3 & =0\\,⇒\\,t=\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $t=-1$ is outside of the domain of $t,$ so we discard it. \n\nThe solution $t=1$ is the only solution that satisfies both equations, so this is the only time when the object is stationary."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186333445 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dccdc3ad-6acb-57ae-83ef-95ffedfbff2f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Velocity Vector of a Particle Moving in Two Dimensions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cb720dfd-53af-583b-b34c-43d9679b51dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v=⟨2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡({t}^{2}-1),4⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333447} {:answer/id #uuid "013dacef-73e2-5f3c-ba74-402ebc078fa2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v=⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡({t}^{2}-1),-4⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333448} {:answer/id #uuid "4b42682f-ace7-5a19-988e-06070c4021b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v=⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡({t}^{2}-1),4⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333449} {:answer/id #uuid "bfb862a6-6085-504a-a189-5cdb10d4f6e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v=⟨-2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡({t}^{2}-1),-4⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333450} {:answer/id #uuid "29c97baa-19fa-5461-8bd0-074df97c32f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v=⟨-2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡({t}^{2}-1),4⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333451}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "29c97baa-19fa-5461-8bd0-074df97c32f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v=⟨-2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡({t}^{2}-1),4⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333451} :answer-field/id #uuid "287ebbd8-c07d-5409-838a-41b78a9b9d19" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333446}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c096fc60-9dd6-554e-aa27-723f03855f59" :question/math-academy-id "q-121709" :question/problem "The position vector $r$ of a particle $P$ (relative to a fixed origin $O$) at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nr=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡({t}^{2}-1),4t-1⟩,\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the velocity vector $v.$" :question/worked-solution "The particle's velocity vector is given by the derivative of its position vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡({t}^{2}-1)),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t-1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡({t}^{2}-1),4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186333452 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ca363e2-c7b7-5196-bd38-10bcc30123ce" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Velocity Vector of a Particle Moving in Two Dimensions at a Given Time"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fb640a6d-7a76-5ec1-a798-704771be4a43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(3)=⟨7,\\frac{5}{16}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333454} {:answer/id #uuid "5ae625d0-7603-54d3-bbfe-7d20ed6b215f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(3)=⟨7,4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333455} {:answer/id #uuid "e823262b-d709-5dca-a52b-ab1a94ec509c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(3)=⟨1,\\frac{5}{16}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333456} {:answer/id #uuid "a2c8f43f-a984-54f6-a907-f85658be243c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(3)=⟨1,4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333457} {:answer/id #uuid "1ecdcea4-4f24-5b43-8d28-0a7494ee797b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(3)=⟨1,\\frac{5}{4}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333458}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e823262b-d709-5dca-a52b-ab1a94ec509c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(3)=⟨1,\\frac{5}{16}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333456} :answer-field/id #uuid "d4053636-baf7-51ec-b9f5-03830a0a55f3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333453}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "03870043-e61d-591f-9542-928769f96294" :question/math-academy-id "q-121719" :question/problem "The position vector $r$ of a particle $P$ (relative to a fixed origin $O$) at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nr=⟨t+4,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5t+1)⟩,\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the velocity vector $v$ at time $t=3.$" :question/worked-solution "The particle's velocity vector is given by the derivative of its position vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+4),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5t+1))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\frac{5}{5t+1}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the velocity when $t=3,$ we substitute into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(3) & =⟨1,\\frac{5}{5(3)+1}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\frac{5}{16}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186333459 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ca363e2-c7b7-5196-bd38-10bcc30123ce" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Velocity Vector of a Particle Moving in Two Dimensions at a Given Time"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b3db8b19-d568-50d0-a489-5717e1bb7dc8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=⟨1,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333461} {:answer/id #uuid "fec23b36-0478-5117-9ca8-0bc7ae7f0246" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=⟨-1,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333462} {:answer/id #uuid "8bac786a-850a-5142-a9d4-89c95623fd9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=⟨1,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333463} {:answer/id #uuid "7e06ad6f-b267-551b-ac40-9fb77d5b3081" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=⟨-1,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333464} {:answer/id #uuid "7ca0f76c-5c2a-580b-86a5-6460adef95ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=⟨0,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333465}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8bac786a-850a-5142-a9d4-89c95623fd9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=⟨1,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333463} :answer-field/id #uuid "47393157-bd26-51b5-95d4-a3633a396640" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333460}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "741539b0-f482-543c-8cda-1da90dd97aaf" :question/math-academy-id "q-121720" :question/problem "The position vector $r$ of a particle $P$ (relative to a fixed origin $O$) at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nr=⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the velocity vector $v$ at time $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "The particle's velocity vector is given by the derivative of its position vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the velocity when $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we substitute into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186333466 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ca363e2-c7b7-5196-bd38-10bcc30123ce" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Velocity Vector of a Particle Moving in Two Dimensions at a Given Time"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d2179c9f-5429-5917-8b1e-4242c9e3b06a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(1)=⟨-5,2e⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333468} {:answer/id #uuid "49ebf0ca-8984-5511-a206-312ad62e8b14" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(1)=⟨-4,2{e}^{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333469} {:answer/id #uuid "fe275af1-2516-5331-9483-e7ad9d3ba716" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(1)=⟨-5,{e}^{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333470} {:answer/id #uuid "ee12e991-cec0-578e-a373-b0bedff2b909" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(1)=⟨-5,2{e}^{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333471} {:answer/id #uuid "42ce0577-1954-5a19-98cb-8ba25f53043e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(1)=⟨-4,2e⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333472}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ee12e991-cec0-578e-a373-b0bedff2b909" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(1)=⟨-5,2{e}^{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333471} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a024740-43c8-54cf-8b82-3c8b939048a6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333467}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "21d59417-64de-5660-afc1-3936a54352ed" :question/math-academy-id "q-121722" :question/problem "The position vector $r$ of a particle $P$ (relative to a fixed origin $O$) at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nr=⟨1-5t,{e}^{2t}⟩,\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the velocity vector $v$ at time $t=1.$" :question/worked-solution "The particle's velocity vector is given by the derivative of its position vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-5t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{2t})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-5,2{e}^{2t}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the velocity when $t=1,$ we substitute into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(1) & =⟨-5,2{e}^{2(1)}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-5,2{e}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186333473 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "11a24997-1f1e-5968-a331-980ca14be2f6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Times at Which a Particle Is Stationary"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08779a6f-b0b9-56ff-8814-a43c29ab9a74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333475} {:answer/id #uuid "cd023b9a-ef5b-5edf-b8dc-e77258dc8f06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=0,4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333476} {:answer/id #uuid "1626e7f9-33d1-54f5-aa99-61752c0c4688" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=0,2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333477} {:answer/id #uuid "a97c7458-8a57-593f-b53b-0777e07d4888" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333478} {:answer/id #uuid "9dc23d8d-070f-50b0-b07c-8adbf9c7ce5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The particle is never stationary" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333479}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a97c7458-8a57-593f-b53b-0777e07d4888" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333478} :answer-field/id #uuid "f1d504dc-d3ab-59bc-b706-2a6beff615f5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333474}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "942f342e-d375-5134-864c-b37213f6cf64" :question/math-academy-id "q-121736" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves in the $x$-$y$ plane (relative to a fixed origin $O$) with position vector\n\n$$\nr=⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡({t}^{2}-4t+8),{t}^{4}-\\frac{8}{3}{t}^{3}⟩,\\,t\\in [0,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nCalculate the times at which the particle is stationary." :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the velocity vector $v.$ Differentiating the position vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({t}^{2}-4t+8),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{4}-\\frac{8}{3}{t}^{3})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2t-4}{{t}^{2}-4t+8},4{t}^{3}-8{t}^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2(t-2)}{{t}^{2}-4t+8},4{t}^{2}(t-2)⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle is stationary when its velocity is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =0 \\\\ ⟨\\frac{2(t-2)}{{t}^{2}-4t+8},4{t}^{2}(t-2)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we need\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2(t-2)}{{t}^{2}-4t+8}=0\\, & ⇒\\,t=2, \\\\ 4{t}^{2}(t-2)=0\\, & ⇒\\,t=0,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution $t=2$ is the only solution that satisfies both equations, so this is the only value of $t$ when the particle is stationary."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186333480 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "11a24997-1f1e-5968-a331-980ca14be2f6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Times at Which a Particle Is Stationary"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9d405f24-ea34-58cd-9307-88b43953de92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=1,2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333482} {:answer/id #uuid "391b90c8-0461-59f1-a74e-1d2b9bb3b81a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333483} {:answer/id #uuid "c2503e2d-37ff-535a-9654-39638b24e106" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333484} {:answer/id #uuid "1d9f3411-f0d0-5426-963b-c243f89c9335" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=0,2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333485} {:answer/id #uuid "443bc0ba-ec31-5637-80e0-f8f036f72645" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The particle is never stationary" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333486}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c2503e2d-37ff-535a-9654-39638b24e106" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333484} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8d7bd5d-e198-549f-8d29-91bff585f890" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333481}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "645e5a65-0546-5aaf-862f-784af9633470" :question/math-academy-id "q-31653" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves in the $x$-$y$ plane (relative to a fixed origin $O$) with position vector\n\n$$\nr=⟨\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1},\\,{t}^{3}-6{t}^{2}⟩,\\,t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the times at which the particle is stationary." :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the velocity vector $v.$ Differentiating the position vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}-6{t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot ({t}^{2}+1)^{-1/2}\\cdot 2t,3{t}^{2}-12t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}},3t(t-4)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle is stationary when its velocity is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =0 \\\\ ⟨\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}},3t(t-4)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we need\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}}=0\\, & ⇒\\,t=0, \\\\ 3t(t-4)=0\\, & ⇒\\,t=0,4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution $t=0$ is the only solution that satisfies both equations, so this is the only value of $t$ when the object is stationary."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308275 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "11a24997-1f1e-5968-a331-980ca14be2f6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Times at Which a Particle Is Stationary"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9dbae6bb-bb43-5bc0-b002-a4e5c364a4c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308277} {:answer/id #uuid "207bcb60-0693-51c7-aba8-96defca8dfd8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308278} {:answer/id #uuid "aae818a5-822e-555c-b2ef-1bed058b0416" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308279} {:answer/id #uuid "01976a4f-3098-54eb-b904-2732ba756ec0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The particle is never stationary" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308280} {:answer/id #uuid "fdd7ee11-39e5-5903-9623-bfb0b41baf09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308281}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "01976a4f-3098-54eb-b904-2732ba756ec0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The particle is never stationary" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308280} :answer-field/id #uuid "333d07b7-e297-52fc-8269-a583519011b1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308276}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "40f57e73-e666-5019-bdd1-73bff2dc4f02" :question/math-academy-id "q-31654" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves in the $x$-$y$ plane (relative to a fixed origin $O$) with position vector\n\n$$\nr=⟨{t}^{3}-3t,\\,2{t}^{2}+6t-3⟩,\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the times at which the particle is stationary." :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the velocity vector $v.$ Differentiating the position vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}-3t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{2}+6t-3)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3{t}^{2}-3,4t+6⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3({t}^{2}-1),2(2t+3)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3(t-1)(t+1),2(2t+3)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle is stationary when its velocity is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =0 \\\\ ⟨3(t-1)(t+1),2(2t+3)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we need\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(t-1)(t+1)=0\\, & ⇒\\,t=\\pm 1, \\\\ 2(2t+3)=0\\, & ⇒\\,t=-\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere is no value of $t$ that satisfies both equations, so there are no stationary points."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186333487 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dccdc3ad-6acb-57ae-83ef-95ffedfbff2f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Velocity Vector of a Particle Moving in Two Dimensions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03d467aa-e370-59e2-b7a5-b35aac35dd27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4}t^2-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333489} {:answer/id #uuid "db690645-92b9-5cea-8e42-95c59acb932e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}t^2-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333490}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "03d467aa-e370-59e2-b7a5-b35aac35dd27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4}t^2-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333489} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f676f38-e312-59f7-ba85-4b0c1db69b74" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333488} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9b6a2436-481a-5434-9a60-e101f08ac652" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{t^4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333492}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9b6a2436-481a-5434-9a60-e101f08ac652" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{t^4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333492} :answer-field/id #uuid "a1df2d10-3c7f-58bc-a7b5-3f08fc76b21a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333491}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "eaa78643-84b2-54f3-b3fd-766531b7411e" :question/math-academy-id "q-352530" :question/problem "The position vector $r$ of a particle $P$ (relative to a fixed origin $O$) at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nr=⟨\\frac{3}{4}{t}^{3}-6t+1,\\,-\\frac{5}{{t}^{3}}⟩,\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the velocity vector $v.$\n\n$v=⟨${{field-1}},\n{{field-2}}$⟩$" :question/worked-solution "The particle's velocity vector is given by the derivative of its position vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{3}{4}{t}^{3}-6t+1),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\frac{5}{{t}^{3}})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{9}{4}{t}^{2}-6,\\frac{15}{{t}^{4}}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186333493 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dccdc3ad-6acb-57ae-83ef-95ffedfbff2f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Velocity Vector of a Particle Moving in Two Dimensions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ef19b771-c203-5816-bd18-f3ba6c885319" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3t}{3t^2+5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333495} {:answer/id #uuid "77b47ecd-3c34-5f7b-acf0-198326c89cc7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6t}{3t^2+5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333496}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "77b47ecd-3c34-5f7b-acf0-198326c89cc7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6t}{3t^2+5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333496} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a8c26bb-e12a-5878-a946-2c4eec2029d6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333494} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fcc54dcc-8725-57ac-90b0-2d9c632bb964" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333498}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fcc54dcc-8725-57ac-90b0-2d9c632bb964" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333498} :answer-field/id #uuid "58b5f17d-55fe-52fd-b472-9b49d5091522" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333497}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c53425b8-ed9c-5281-b0c0-87690aa337af" :question/math-academy-id "q-352536" :question/problem "The position vector $r$ of a particle $P$ (relative to a fixed origin $O$) at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nr=⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3{t}^{2}+5),4-2t⟩,\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the velocity vector $v.$\n\n$v=⟨${{field-1}},\n{{field-2}}$⟩$" :question/worked-solution "The particle's velocity vector is given by the derivative of its position vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3{t}^{2}+5)),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4-2t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{6t}{3{t}^{2}+5},-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186333499 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dccdc3ad-6acb-57ae-83ef-95ffedfbff2f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Velocity Vector of a Particle Moving in Two Dimensions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b1a3600b-913f-597f-8bcc-4fd475077119" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2t}{t^2+4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333501}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b1a3600b-913f-597f-8bcc-4fd475077119" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2t}{t^2+4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333501} :answer-field/id #uuid "e38dae9d-3466-506a-8ef1-3a92aca8c778" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333500}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5895ef0d-c063-5025-ae65-53030ec3bfea" :question/math-academy-id "q-352538" :question/problem "The position vector $r$ of a particle $P$ (relative to a fixed origin $O$) at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nr=⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7-3{t}^{2}),\\operatorname{ln}⁡({t}^{2}+4)⟩,\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the velocity vector $v.$\n\n$v=⟨\\,-\\frac{6t}{7-3{t}^{2}},$\n{{field-1}}$⟩$" :question/worked-solution "The particle's velocity vector is given by the derivative of its position vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7-3{t}^{2})),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({t}^{2}+4))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{6t}{7-3{t}^{2}},\\frac{2t}{{t}^{2}+4}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186333502 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dccdc3ad-6acb-57ae-83ef-95ffedfbff2f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Velocity Vector of a Particle Moving in Two Dimensions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02d26881-85f2-52b3-8728-2b2e2b235db9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3t^2+2}{t^3+2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333504}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "02d26881-85f2-52b3-8728-2b2e2b235db9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3t^2+2}{t^3+2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333504} :answer-field/id #uuid "334c5d00-be75-5ac0-9a97-bcbe80a1f88f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333503}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d3593e3a-62f3-5ba1-ba98-5a7176a17945" :question/math-academy-id "q-352539" :question/problem "The position vector $r$ of a particle $P$ (relative to a fixed origin $O$) at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nr=⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡({t}^{3}+2t),\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9-{t}^{2})⟩,\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the velocity vector $v.$\n\n$v=⟨${{field-1}}$,\\,-\\frac{2t}{9-{t}^{2}}\\,⟩$" :question/worked-solution "The particle's velocity vector is given by the derivative of its position vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({t}^{3}+2t)),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9-{t}^{2}))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{3{t}^{2}+2}{{t}^{3}+2t},-\\frac{2t}{9-{t}^{2}}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186333505 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ca363e2-c7b7-5196-bd38-10bcc30123ce" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Velocity Vector of a Particle Moving in Two Dimensions at a Given Time"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7edc0ef2-3374-5396-bf68-fe6d96efaf91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333507} {:answer/id #uuid "8c132b5e-7bda-554f-bc5b-a5c02cc0c708" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333508}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8c132b5e-7bda-554f-bc5b-a5c02cc0c708" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333508} :answer-field/id #uuid "16d9c637-816b-5e0f-aadd-9066f3e555aa" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333506} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6c292866-73c9-5d3b-a563-fa23a15c6a9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333510}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6c292866-73c9-5d3b-a563-fa23a15c6a9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333510} :answer-field/id #uuid "bce0b200-17e2-503f-95eb-67ac0ce7ad5b" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333509}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6a005320-38e4-5c29-a628-b544e5ffac11" :question/math-academy-id "q-352570" :question/problem "The position vector $r$ of a particle $P$ (relative to a fixed origin $O$) at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nr=⟨5\\sqrt{1+8t},\\,(3+2t)^{3/2}⟩,\\,0<t<4.\n$$\n\nCalculate the components of the velocity vector $v$ at time $t=3.$ Do not round the answers.\n\n$v(3)=⟨${{field-1}},\n{{field-2}}$⟩$" :question/worked-solution "The particle's velocity vector is given by the derivative of its position vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5\\sqrt{1+8t}),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}((3+2t)^{3/2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}(1+8t)^{-1/2}\\cdot 8,\\frac{3}{2}(3+2t)^{1/2}\\cdot 2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{20}{\\sqrt{1+8t}},3\\sqrt{3+2t}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the velocity when $t=3,$ we substitute into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(3) & =⟨\\frac{20}{\\sqrt{1+8(3)}},3\\sqrt{3+2(3)}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{20}{\\sqrt{25}},3\\sqrt{9}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{20}{5},3(3)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4,\\,9⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186333511 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "646467d1-f764-5a3c-b03b-7d74e588611d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Speed of a Particle at a Given Time"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d5835d4-8696-554f-bb70-a2bda4ac2d94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{26}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333513}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4d5835d4-8696-554f-bb70-a2bda4ac2d94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{26}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333513} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e8ceebc-1b05-539e-91d2-8fbd23848387" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333512}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "810e6c21-1003-5a47-b790-30b10392715b" :question/math-academy-id "q-352572" :question/problem "The position vector $r$ of a particle $P$ (relative to a fixed origin $O$) at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nr=⟨\\frac{5{t}^{2}}{2},\\,3t+2⟩,\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the speed  $|v|$ of the particle at time $t=3.$ Do not round the answer.\n\nThe speed at time $t=3$ is\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the velocity vector $v.$ Differentiating the position vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{5{t}^{2}}{2}),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t+2)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5t,\\,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the velocity when $t=3,$ we substitute in the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(3) & =⟨5(3),\\,3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨15,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the speed at $t=3$ we calculate the magnitude of $v(3),$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|v(3)| & =\\sqrt{{15}^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{225+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{234} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{26}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186333514 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "646467d1-f764-5a3c-b03b-7d74e588611d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Speed of a Particle at a Given Time"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fc38d2dc-1527-56e3-96af-51823830504a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333516}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fc38d2dc-1527-56e3-96af-51823830504a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333516} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec00e37a-ad51-598d-b18a-2ef6678ae717" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333515}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b661ab3f-53c8-53bd-9aca-1b6a65f8f496" :question/math-academy-id "q-352584" :question/problem "The position vector $r$ of a particle $P$ (relative to a fixed origin $O$) at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nr=⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+2),\\,\\frac{4}{t}⟩,\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the speed $|v|$ of the particle at time $t=2.$ Do not round the answer.\n\nThe speed at time $t=2$ is\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the velocity vector $v.$ Differentiating the position vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+2)),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{4}{t})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{t+2},\\,-\\frac{4}{{t}^{2}}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the velocity when $t=2,$ we substitute in the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(2) & =⟨\\frac{1}{2+2},\\,-\\frac{4}{{2}^{2}}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{4},\\,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the speed at $t=2$ we calculate the magnitude of $v(2),$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|v(2)| & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}+{(-1)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{16}+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{17}{16}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186333517 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "646467d1-f764-5a3c-b03b-7d74e588611d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Speed of a Particle at a Given Time"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d720b213-48a4-514d-bb6a-de868f54160f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333519}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d720b213-48a4-514d-bb6a-de868f54160f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333519} :answer-field/id #uuid "42b67214-0fe8-5675-94ac-cef6f791a1f7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333518}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "edb7fca2-61e2-5800-a4c9-2d1de8e94995" :question/math-academy-id "q-352588" :question/problem "The position vector $r$ of a particle $P$ (relative to a fixed origin $O$) at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nr=⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+3),\\,\\sqrt{t}⟩,\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the speed $|v|$ of the particle at time $t=1.$ Do not round the answer.\n\nThe speed at time $t=1$ is\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the velocity vector $v.$ Differentiating the position vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+3)),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{t})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{t+3},\\,\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the velocity when $t=1,$ we substitute in the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(1) & =⟨\\frac{1}{1+3},\\,\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{1}}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{4},\\,\\frac{1}{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the speed at $t=1$ we calculate the magnitude of $v(1),$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|v(1)| & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{16}+\\frac{1}{4}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{5}{16}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186333520 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "646467d1-f764-5a3c-b03b-7d74e588611d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Speed of a Particle at a Given Time"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "364e4a22-93a9-5ccc-8699-62a04f227ce1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333522} {:answer/id #uuid "2027ea17-89db-5e33-aa05-0177b2f0450a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333523}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2027ea17-89db-5e33-aa05-0177b2f0450a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333523} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f449c06-653a-5e84-804c-c7eb77aa2bd9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333521}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "def01361-ad83-523b-8ea1-25745a466c56" :question/math-academy-id "q-352596" :question/problem "A particle $P$ is moving in the $x$-$y$ plane. Its position vector relative to a fixed origin $O$ is given by\n\n$$\nr=⟨4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}t),\\,\\sqrt{t+2}⟩,\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the speed of the particle at time $t=2.$\n\nThe speed at time $t=2$ is\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the velocity vector $v.$ Differentiating the position vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}t)),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{t+2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}t),\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t+2}}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the velocity when $t=2,$ we substitute in the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(2) & =⟨-2\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi ),\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2+2}}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\frac{1}{4}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the speed at $t=2$ we calculate the magnitude of $v(2),$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|v(2)| & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186333524 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "11a24997-1f1e-5968-a331-980ca14be2f6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Times at Which a Particle Is Stationary"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5eb323b7-2a9c-5e57-9d96-8d03a0fc8aaf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333526}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5eb323b7-2a9c-5e57-9d96-8d03a0fc8aaf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333526} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d7baf41-bc4f-55f5-add8-231725817a72" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333525}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "407b9aae-8296-546a-aea3-e0d4a26f0781" :question/math-academy-id "q-352729" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves in the $x$-$y$ plane (relative to a fixed origin $O$) with position vector\n\n$$\nr=⟨t-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)⟩,\\,t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$\n\nCalculate the times at which the particle is stationary.\n\nThe particle is stationary at $t=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the velocity vector $v.$ Differentiating the position vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle is stationary when its velocity is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =0 \\\\ ⟨1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we need\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t=0\\, & ⇒\\,t=0, \\\\ -3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t=0\\, & ⇒\\,t=0,\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution $t=0$ is the only solution that satisfies both equations, so this is the only value of $t$ when the object is stationary."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186333527 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "11a24997-1f1e-5968-a331-980ca14be2f6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Times at Which a Particle Is Stationary"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a1b2b9f-00d2-53fa-925c-73024cdc4644" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333529}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3a1b2b9f-00d2-53fa-925c-73024cdc4644" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333529} :answer-field/id #uuid "7463eb62-799a-59e0-9808-ba9cc58ef7f4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333528}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fea150a4-e472-5de1-95f5-af02e8f830f9" :question/math-academy-id "q-352732" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves in the $x$-$y$ plane (relative to a fixed origin $O$) with position vector\n\n$$\nr=⟨t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}⟩,\\,t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$\n\nCalculate the times at which the particle is stationary.\n\nThe particle is stationary at $t=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the velocity vector $v.$ Differentiating the position vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle is stationary when its velocity is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =0 \\\\ ⟨1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}}⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we need\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=0\\, & ⇒\\,t=0, \\\\ \\frac{t}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}}=0\\, & ⇒\\,t=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution $t=0$ is the only solution that satisfies both equations, so this is the only value of $t$ when the object is stationary."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186333530 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ca363e2-c7b7-5196-bd38-10bcc30123ce" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Velocity Vector of a Particle Moving in Two Dimensions at a Given Time"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "418df1f8-c1b6-5814-93ab-50d1f6b8bf52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(3)=⟨1,\\,7⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333532} {:answer/id #uuid "3fd6f126-ee0b-58dc-8cc4-6a3bb77cbc5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(3)=⟨4,\\,8⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333533} {:answer/id #uuid "c224322a-eb76-5047-a8c8-09047a630b86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(3)=⟨2,\\,7⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333534} {:answer/id #uuid "7759ed35-5b2e-5585-bf80-e5d8a0e8ebf7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(3)=⟨2,\\,8⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333535} {:answer/id #uuid "688c6f84-12da-5c59-8a7b-1ecf2bfff4b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(3)=⟨1,\\,8⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333536}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "688c6f84-12da-5c59-8a7b-1ecf2bfff4b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(3)=⟨1,\\,8⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333536} :answer-field/id #uuid "20395fba-119a-5a5f-ac76-ddd929d57598" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333531}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6f58a47e-55ad-5bda-ac53-f0cf87e9e705" :question/math-academy-id "q-75290" :question/problem "The position vector $r$ of a particle $P$ (relative to a fixed origin $O$) at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nr=⟨4\\sqrt{1+t},(1+t)^{2}⟩,\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the velocity vector $v$ at time $t=3.$" :question/worked-solution "The particle's velocity vector is given by the derivative of its position vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\sqrt{1+t}),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}((1+t)^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2(1+t)^{-1/2},2(1+t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{1+t}},2+2t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the velocity when $t=3,$ we substitute into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(3) & =⟨\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{1+3}},2+2(3)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2}{2},\\,2+6⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,8⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186333537 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "646467d1-f764-5a3c-b03b-7d74e588611d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Speed of a Particle at a Given Time"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6749f6f8-90f6-5481-a85a-58d5822e6356" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333539} {:answer/id #uuid "e71e3848-d37d-56a9-8db2-ba08016c2a8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333540} {:answer/id #uuid "5058623a-1137-569d-ac5c-32bf494eea2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333541} {:answer/id #uuid "a06432fe-fa44-5a17-bc32-2d7362adabfa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333542} {:answer/id #uuid "3cd015fc-178b-5c8f-876d-ea9bc050f144" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333543}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3cd015fc-178b-5c8f-876d-ea9bc050f144" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333543} :answer-field/id #uuid "d180a47c-8720-55e6-8def-33ab730913c2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333538}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "71df76b4-b01c-58e6-a008-71b6cfb97015" :question/math-academy-id "q-75302" :question/problem "A particle $P$ is moving in the $x$-$y$ plane. Its position vector relative to a fixed origin $O$ is given by\n\n$$\nr=⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)⟩,\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the speed of the particle at time $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the velocity vector $v.$ Differentiating the position vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the velocity when $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we substitute in the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =⟨-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi ),4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi )⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-4(0),4(1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the speed at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ we calculate the magnitude of $v(\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|v(\\frac{\\pi }{2})| & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
